+
+[DEPENDENCIES: TWO SOURCES]
+  remote -- the normal case: a package a Conan remote carries is declared once, by version, in
+            conandata.yml.
+  local  -- a package no remote carries keeps its CCI-SHAPED RECIPE in the tree, under
+            .github/project/local-packages/<name>/{config.yml, all/conandata.yml, all/conanfile.py,
+            all/test_package/}, and is described in .github/project/package-sources.json.
+            `python3 .github/project/package_sources.py` (no arguments) exports and builds it for
+            the configuration that asks for it.
+            A recipe, not a built artifact: it builds from source per platform, so Linux, macOS,
+            Windows and every cross profile get a package for their own configuration, and no
+            binary is bound to the machine that committed it. conandata.yml does carry the
+            upstream URL and sha256 -- the same thing CCI commits.
+
+[CONDITIONAL DEPENDENCIES]
+  A dependency that exists only while a switch is on is ONE entry in
+  metadata.json:optional_dependencies. The key is the package name; the entry carries `option`
+  (name / default on|off / defines as macro-to-value), `targets`, and `source`. One entry answers
+  both 'when' and 'what', so a package never needs a second half in another table: `source: local`
+  says a stock CCI remote cannot fetch it, so an online CI leg keeps that option off.
+  conanfile.py, test_package/conanfile.py, CMakeLists.txt and the schema read that one table with
+  one rule; nobody edits a build file to add an option. Precedence: a Conan `-o <option>=...` run
+  overrides the file's `default`, because it is a per-run choice.
